## Keep identity separate from meaning

The selected [interface palette](../styles/colour.html) controls presentation. A family crest or business logo does not redefine errors, warnings, focus or chart series. Do not recolour supplied artwork when a palette changes; use a suitable neutral background instead.

Use authorised artwork and preserve its meaning. Do not copy a government mark or imply endorsement. The crest and business symbol in these examples are original illustrative shapes, not a claim to a registered coat of arms or a real business identity.

Keep names and state descriptions as real text. When an image repeats the adjacent name, use empty alternative text; when it conveys otherwise missing identity, supply a concise name. Never place essential controls or readings inside the image.

## Check a personalised interface

- A text-only option works fully, including when images are unavailable.
- Long, Welsh and multilingual names remain readable without clipping.
- The home link has a meaningful name and destination and a visible keyboard focus.
- Light and dark surfaces retain readable names and unchanged artwork.
- Shared displays reveal only the identity the household chose to show; no address or full personal name is required.
- Removing personalisation leaves controls, chart meanings, permissions and device names unchanged.

Use the shared [header](../components/header.html), [colour roles](../styles/brand-colour.html) and [image guidance](../styles/images.html) when implementing this specification.
